home='<main id="main"><section class="hero shell"><div class="hero-identity"><p class="eyebrow">Cornell Information Science · May 2027</p><h1>I’m Beryl Koko.</h1><p class="disciplines">Product. Data. Design.<br><span>And the technical work to build it.</span></p></div><div class="hero-description"><p>Most of my projects start with something confusing, inefficient, or interesting that I want to understand. Sometimes that becomes an analysis, sometimes a redesign, and sometimes a product I can build.</p><a class="work-jump" href="#work">Explore my work <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div></section><section id="work" class="shell work-section"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Selected work <span>/ 05</span></h2><div class="work-filters" aria-label="Filter selected work" hidden><button type="button" data-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All work</button><button type="button" data-filter="product" aria-pressed="false">Product</button><button type="button" data-filter="data" aria-pressed="false">Data</button><button type="button" data-filter="design" aria-pressed="false">Design</button></div></div><p class="sr-only" id="filter-status" aria-live="polite"></p><div class="project-grid">'+cards+'</div></section><section id="about" class="about shell"><div><p class="eyebrow">How I work</p><h2>I like figuring out<br>what’s underneath.</h2><a class="text-link" href="assets/beryl-koko-resume.pdf">Read my résumé ↗</a></div><div class="about-copy"><p>A confusing race graphic. A book search that misses what I mean. A claim about a driver that feels too simple. Those are the kinds of questions that keep me looking.</p><p>Across these projects, I work through the question, the evidence, the design choices and the implementation. I’m interested in product management, product analysis, and roles where data and design help shape what gets built.</p><div class="skill-lines"><div><b>Understand</b><span>Research · Interface analysis · Product questions</span></div><div><b>Analyze</b><span>Python · SQL · Data visualization</span></div><div><b>Build</b><span>Prototypes · Web interfaces · API-backed products</span></div></div></div></section></main>'
(P/'index.html').write_text(page('Product, data & design','Beryl Koko, Cornell Information Science student graduating May 2027. Explore product, data analysis, UX research and technical work.',home))
content={}
content['mcqueen']=section('01 / The question','Has Hamilton declined—or has the baseline distorted the story?',p('Winning became so routine during the Mercedes years that later results could look like a simple decline. This project asks what changes when that impression is checked against career outcome rates, teammates and constructor context.'))+fig('mcqueen-hero-new.jpg','The live editorial opening gives the question a face before introducing the evidence. Lewis Hamilton at the 2008 British GP; photography by Marc Evans, CC BY-SA 2.0.')+section('02 / My contribution','From a career question to a reproducible argument.',p('Defined the comparison, assembled season-level data, documented sources and head-to-head conventions, wrote the Python analysis, and built the interactive HTML/CSS/Chart.js story. The finished scope is career results from 2007–2026 YTD—not telemetry or a mechanical diagnosis.'))+pair(fig('mcqueen-rates.jpg','Actual career-rates chart: wins, podiums and poles as a share of Grand Prix starts.'),'A fairer unit of comparison.', 'Season lengths vary. Rates per Grand Prix start make seasons comparable without letting a longer calendar inflate the result. The 2014–2020 Mercedes championship run provides an explicit peak baseline: 73 wins from 137 starts (53.3%).')+pair(fig('mcqueen-team.jpg','Constructor position sits beside Hamilton’s win totals in the finished story.'),'Put the machinery back in the picture.','Raw trophies cannot isolate a driver. Constructor position adds team context; qualifying, race head-to-heads and teammate-pair points share add another comparison. Neither is a perfect control for reliability, setup, strategy or adaptation.')+section('03 / Reading the evidence','The simple decline story does not hold up.',p('The project finds a clear drop from the peak, with weaker recent teammate comparisons—especially qualifying in 2024–2025. Its 2026 YTD snapshot also shows a rebound against Leclerc. These are descriptive comparisons, not proof that one factor caused the change.'))+section('04 / The experience','Let the reader follow the argument.', '<div class="decision-list"><div><h3>Start with the peak</h3><p>Large baseline figures make the unusually high reference point visible.</p></div><div><h3>Move from career to context</h3><p>The sequence narrows from outcome rates to constructors, then teammates, before giving the conclusion.</p></div><div><h3>Keep charts explorable</h3><p>Hover values and selectable Chart.js legends let readers inspect the lines; photography and short chapters break up the analysis.</p></div></div>')+section('05 / Scope & sources','An argument with its limits attached.',p('The repository documents race-duel rules, sprint-inclusive championship points and an incomplete 2026 season. The Python script and CSVs make the baseline calculation reproducible. The final story deliberately excludes unsupported telemetry and performance-degradation claims.')+'<a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/BerylKoko/F1-Analysis/blob/main/data/SOURCES.md">Read data sources & conventions ↗</a>')
content['race-lens']=section('01 / Starting point','Seeing the race is not the same as understanding it.',p('F1 interfaces contain an enormous amount of useful information. The design question was how a newer viewer could understand gaps, tyres and pit stops without first learning the conventions of a timing wall.'))+fig('race-lens-new.jpg','Race Lens: a spatial replay, running order and contextual driver focus. All race data and track geometry are illustrative.')+section('02 / My contribution','Research, interaction design and the working prototype.',p('The work spans existing-interface analysis, a task-based interview protocol, design exploration and implementation in HTML, CSS and JavaScript. The research artifacts below show observations and questions that informed the design; they are not a record of measured usability improvements.'))+pair(fig('f1-timing.png','Annotated timing-table analysis from Existing F1 Interfaces.'),'A number needs a reference point.','The timing-table analysis questions the distinction between interval and gap to the leader, unfamiliar abbreviations and rapidly changing values. Race Lens makes the reference explicit: “seconds behind leader,” with names alongside driver codes.')+pair(fig('f1-tracker.png','Annotated F1TV driver-tracker analysis.'),'A map gives orientation, not the whole answer.','Spatial proximity can be misleading during pit cycles or when cars are lapped. The prototype pairs its spatial field with a running-order list and a selected-driver panel, so the map is not the only way to read the race.')+pair(fig('f1-tyres.png','Tyre-history interface analysis: compound, colour and age.'),'Explain what the tyre label means.','Compound colours help recognition, but they do not explain the pace–durability trade-off. Driver focus combines the compound name, age in laps, pit status and a short plain-language explanation.')+pair(fig('f1-dashboard.png','Graphprix pitwall analysis: useful detail, dense presentation.'),'Give the viewer somewhere to start.','The analysis identifies an expert-oriented density of panels. Race Lens begins with the field and a race snapshot; selecting a driver brings the relevant timing and tyre context into focus.')+section('03 / Research artifact','Turn broad confusion into concrete tasks.',p('The interview protocol asks a novice to interpret timing gaps, sector colours, tyre age, map battles, pit re-entry and safety signals, then identify what attracts attention in a brief glance. These tasks make “is this understandable?” specific enough to investigate.')+'<div class="artifact-links"><a href="{ROOT}assets/f1-interview-protocol.pdf">Open interview protocol ↗</a><a href="{ROOT}assets/f1-interface-analysis.pdf">Open annotated interface analysis ↗</a></div>')+fig('interview-task-3.png','A page from the task-based interview protocol. This is the research instrument, not participant findings.')+section('04 / Design into interaction','One replay state, several ways to understand it.','<div class="decision-list"><div><h3>Control the pace</h3><p>Play, scrub across 50 illustrative laps, or jump to lap 24 to inspect a pit-stop transition.</p></div><div><h3>Choose the relevant detail</h3><p>Select a driver from the map, selector or running order. Tyre summaries and pit progress keep the wider field in view.</p></div><div><h3>Compare without mental arithmetic</h3><p>Two driver selectors produce side-by-side timing, compound, tyre age and pit status, plus a sentence describing the gap.</p></div></div>')+fig('race-lens-comparison.jpg','The implemented comparison at illustrative lap 24: tyre age, pit status and the gap are readable together.')+section('05 / Implementation','A finished interaction prototype.',p('JavaScript derives the displayed lap, tyre changes, tyre age and comparison text from the illustrative driver data. The SVG track, driver markers, sidebar and comparison panel update together. This demonstrates the interaction model; it does not simulate or reproduce real race telemetry.'))
content['job-market']=section('01 / The question','What are these jobs actually asking for?',p('Analyst, product and BI titles overlap, but they do not necessarily describe the same entry points or skill requirements. This analysis looks at role volume, experience labels, salaries and tool mentions within a defined job-posting sample.'))+'<div class="stat-strip"><div><strong>14,681</strong><span>source postings</span></div><div><strong>188</strong><span>target-role postings</span></div><div><strong>5</strong><span>role families</span></div></div>'+pair(fig('role_volume.svg','Role counts from the repository’s current analysis.'),'Define the sample before comparing it.','A conservative, rule-based title classifier identifies Business Analyst, Product Management, Data Analyst, Business Intelligence and Product Analyst postings. Only two Product Analyst postings qualify, so that group cannot support a broad claim.')+section('02 / My contribution','A reproducible analysis, from tables to findings.',p('Built the notebook workflow, relational schema and example SQL queries, joined job-to-skill and job-to-industry tables, and presented the findings visually. The notebook uses pandas joins; the SQL files document the relational structure and query approach. Missing labels are retained, and salary filtering is limited to the salary analysis.'))+pair(fig('entry_level_share.svg','Entry-level share by role, using the full role-group denominator.'),'The experience mix changes the comparison.','28 of the 188 target postings are labelled entry level (14.9%). Data Analyst has the largest entry-level share: 19 of 45 postings (42.2%). Experience labels are missing for 48 postings, so this is a description of this sample, not the entire hiring market.')+fig('skill_keywords.svg','Literal description keyword matches across 188 target postings. SQL appears in 55 (29.3%); Excel in 43 (22.9%).')+section('03 / Analytical decisions','Keep different kinds of evidence separate.','<div class="decision-list"><div><h3>Tags ≠ keyword mentions</h3><p>Relational skill categories and words found in descriptions answer different questions. Multi-valued associations are not distinct job counts.</p></div><div><h3>Salary needs its denominator</h3><p>Salary medians use only usable normalized values. Different seniority mixes and small groups prevent a clean role-to-role pay conclusion.</p></div><div><h3>Descriptive, not representative</h3><p>Rule-based titles and literal keyword matching miss some meanings. The sample is a useful lens, not a census of demand.</p></div></div>')+'<div class="artifact-links"><a href="https://github.com/BerylKoko/Job-Market-Skills-Analysis">Explore notebook, schema & SQL ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/BerylKoko/Job-Market-Skills-Analysis/blob/main/results/findings.md">Read findings & limitations ↗</a></div>'
content['bookmatch']=section('01 / Starting point','A reader knows more than a book title.',p('Sometimes the request is a title or an author. Sometimes it is a combination of subjects, settings or tropes. Bookmatch explores how to turn that intent into browsable results with enough context to decide what to read next.'))+fig('bookmatch-results.jpg','The deployed book-discovery experience: results with covers, book metadata and actions.')+pair(fig('bookmatch-search.jpg','Search and discovery entry points in the live product.'),'Keep discovery close to the search.','The interface supports title, author and subject searches, with a separate preference-led discovery flow. Results can be inspected in Details and saved to a local reading list, keeping the path from browsing to a next read short.')+pair(fig('bookmatch-details.jpg','A book’s Details view in the deployed application.'),'Make the result useful before the click out.','Book metadata and a reading/source link let readers assess a result without starting over elsewhere. Missing covers and incomplete metadata are expected API conditions, so the implementation includes fallbacks instead of assuming a perfect catalogue.')+fig('bookmatch-reading-list.jpg','Saved books in the browser’s local reading list; no account is required.')+section('02 / My contribution','The product experience and the implementation behind it.',p('Designed and built the Flask application and JavaScript interface, connecting book-catalog data to search, subject/trope discovery, recommendation ranking, book details and a persistent reading list. The work includes the product flow, API handling, metadata normalization and the logic used to assess a match.'))+section('03 / Recommendation decisions','Retrieval is not proof of a match.','<div class="pipeline"><div><b>01</b><h3>Retrieve candidates</h3><p>Bounded query variants cover related vocabulary. Sports queries expand into concrete sports.</p></div><div><b>02</b><h3>Combine metadata</h3><p>Merge by ISBN or matching title and author; preserve richer descriptions, covers and reading links.</p></div><div><b>03</b><h3>Check the evidence</h3><p>Descriptions and targeted relevance inform concept matches. Fuzzy query membership alone does not prove an M/M or F/F pairing.</p></div><div><b>04</b><h3>Rank & explain</h3><p>Full matches rank ahead of partial ones. Evidence and missing-concept fields describe the result’s fit.</p></div></div>')+section('04 / Technical choices','Build for imperfect external data.',p('The implementation separates catalog retrieval from normalization and ranking. Cached requests reduce repeated work; metadata and cover fallbacks account for incomplete records. Query and page validation, readable API errors and regression tests cover the less visible parts of the experience: duplicate records, exact-title relevance, false-positive pairings, pagination and bounded retrieval.'))+section('05 / What this work demonstrates','A result the reader can understand and keep.',p('The finished interface connects discovery, browsing, details and saving in one flow. Author and year filters narrow the results; pagination keeps larger result sets manageable; a browser-persisted reading list makes it easy to return to a book. Match explanations make the recommendation logic inspectable, including where catalogue evidence is incomplete.'))
content['harvest']=section('01 / The experience','Help someone picture the visit—and plan it.',p('An event website has to convey the atmosphere while answering practical questions: what is happening, who is there and how to get there. This responsive student project brings those needs into a single festival guide.'))+fig('harvest-home.jpg','The finished homepage uses warm colour, serif type and festival photography to establish the setting.')+pair(fig('harvest-lineup-new.jpg','The lineup page combines a scannable schedule with vendor photography.'),'Separate the visitor’s questions.','Activities, lineup, gallery, information and directions each have a clear destination in the navigation. The lineup combines time-based information with visual context; the directions page brings transport details and maps together.')+section('02 / Design artifacts','Work out the layout at both sizes.',p('The repository includes wide and narrow layout sketches and iteration artifacts. They show the page hierarchy before implementation: navigation, a large event introduction and supporting content that can stack on smaller screens.'))+'<div class="two-up">'+fig('wide-layout-sketch.jpeg','Original wide-layout sketch from the project repository.')+fig('narrow-layout-sketch.jpeg','Original narrow-layout sketch from the project repository.')+'</div>'+section('03 / My contribution','Visual design, responsive layout and front-end behaviour.',p('Created the page layouts and implemented the HTML, CSS and JavaScript across the guide. The responsive navigation exposes its expanded state and closes with Escape. The tips interaction uses a native dialog and restores focus to its trigger when closed. Instructor-provided content and photography are credited in the source.'))+section('04 / Finished scope','A complete event-guide website.',p('The result is a multi-page, responsive frontend with a consistent visual language and purposeful navigation. It is an archived student design project using historical event material—not the festival’s official or current information service.'))
for i,proj in enumerate(projects):
 slug,title,tags,desc,img,live,repo=proj
 nextp=projects[(i+1)%5]
 body=f'<main id="main"><div class="case-heading shell"><a class="back" href="../../index.html#work">← Selected work</a><div class="case-intro"><div><p class="eyebrow">{tags}</p><h1>{title}</h1><p class="case-dek">{desc}</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="{live}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View live project ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/BerylKoko/{repo}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub / source ↗</a></div></div><a class="case-preview zoom" href="../../assets/{img}" aria-label="Enlarge {title} preview"><img src="../../assets/{img}" alt="{title} interface" fetchpriority="high"></a></div></div><div class="case-body shell">{content[slug]}</div><section class="next-project shell"><div class="next-header"><h2>More work</h2><a href="../../index.html#work">All projects</a></div><a class="next-card" href="../{nextp[0]}/index.html"><img src="../../assets/{nextp[4]}" alt="{nextp[1]} preview" loading="lazy"><div><p class="eyebrow">{nextp[2]}</p><h3>{nextp[1]}</h3><p>{nextp[3]}</p><span class="card-cta">Explore case study →</span></div></a></section></main>'

 d=P/'work'/slug;d.mkdir(exist_ok=True);(d/'index.html').write_text(page(title,desc,body,'../../','case '+slug))
